# Yamaha MTX/MRX/XMV/EXi8/EXo8 Driver
#
# Controls Yamaha MTX3, MTX5-D, MRX7-D matrix processors and the XMV / EXi8 /
# EXo8 expansion devices over the Yamaha Remote Control Protocol (RCP).
#
# Transport: TCP, port 49280, line-based ASCII, LF-terminated.
# Source:    "MTX3, MTX5-D, MRX7-D, XMV Series, EXi8, EXo8 Remote Control
#            Protocol Specifications V4.0.0 rev14" (Yamaha, 2020-03-30)
# https://usa.yamaha.com/files/download/other_assets/5/1343735/200330_mtx_mrx_xmv_ex_rcps_v400_rev14_en.pdf
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Push vs poll (per protocol spec):
#   The protocol is push-capable. The device automatically sends NOTIFY
#   messages on parameter changes, preset/snapshot recalls, run-mode changes,
#   sample-rate changes, etc. NO explicit "subscribe" command is needed -
#   NOTIFY is unsolicited and global. The driver listens for NOTIFY lines and
#   updates state immediately. Polling is a refresh backstop for liveness
#   detection (devstatus runmode) and to backfill state at connect.
#
# Address model:
#   MRX7-D parameters are addressed by an "Index" assigned in the Remote
#   Control Setup List (configured in MTX-MRX Editor). The integrator wires
#   their faders / mutes / selectors to Index 1..N, then this driver controls
#   them by Index number - the Symetrix Composer "controller number" model.
#   This driver exposes Index 1..32 (covers typical installations).
#
#   MTX3 / MTX5-D / XMV / EXi8 / EXo8 use full parameter paths
#   (MTX:mem_MemNo/UniqueId/ElmNo/Xpos/Ypos/PrmNo/IndexNo). The driver exposes
#   generic set_param / get_param / inc_param commands for those devices, and
#   surfaces the most-recent response in last_param_* state vars.
#
#   Both addressing modes coexist on the wire; an integrator can mix them in
#   one project by binding panel controls to either Index commands or raw
#   set_param commands.
#
# Authentication:
#   The protocol has no authentication. RCP must be enabled in MTX-MRX Editor
#   (System -> Remote Control). Up to 8 simultaneous network sessions per
#   device.
#
# Keepalive:
#   The protocol supports an optional keepalive timeout (scpmode keepalive).
#   Disabled by default in the device. Most installations don't need it; if
#   enabled, the device closes the connection if no command/heart-beat arrives
#   within the configured timeout. Driver default leaves it off.


id: yamaha_mtx_mrx
name: Yamaha MTX/MRX/XMV
manufacturer: Yamaha
category: audio
version: 1.1.0
author: OpenAVC
description: >
  Controls Yamaha install-audio processors (MTX3, MTX5-D, MRX7-D) and the
  XMV power amps + EXi8 / EXo8 audio expanders over the Remote Control
  Protocol (RCP) on TCP port 49280. Push-capable: NOTIFY messages from the
  device drive state immediately; polling backstops liveness and refresh.
  MRX7-D parameters are addressed via Remote Control Setup List Index numbers
  (1..32); MTX/XMV use full parameter paths via generic set_param / get_param.

source_url: https://usa.yamaha.com/files/download/other_assets/5/1343735/200330_mtx_mrx_xmv_ex_rcps_v400_rev14_en.pdf
tags: [dsp, install-audio, matrix-processor, dante, paging, ducking, zone-mixer, rcp]
verified: false
simulated: true
protocols: [yamaha_rcp]
ports: [49280]
compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: Yamaha
    models:
      - MTX3
      - MTX5-D
      - MRX7-D
    confidence: untested
    notes: >
      Matrix processors. MRX7-D requires a Remote Control Setup List in
      MTX-MRX Editor that maps user faders / mutes / selectors to Index
      numbers; this driver controls them by Index. MTX3 / MTX5-D use
      full parameter addresses via the generic set_param command.
  - manufacturer: Yamaha
    models:
      - XMV4140
      - XMV4140-D
      - XMV4280
      - XMV4280-D
      - XMV8140
      - XMV8140-D
      - XMV8280
      - XMV8280-D
      - XMV16140
    confidence: untested
    notes: >
      XMV power amps. Controlled via full parameter paths (set_param /
      get_param). RCP control is independent of any MTX/MRX in the system.
  - manufacturer: Yamaha
    models:
      - EXi8
      - EXo8
    confidence: untested
    notes: >
      Audio I/O expanders. Same RCP address model as XMV.

transport: tcp
delimiter: "\n"

help:
  overview: >
    Yamaha RCP is the unified ASCII control protocol used across the MTX,
    MRX, XMV, EXi8, and EXo8 product family. The protocol is push-capable -
    the device sends a NOTIFY message whenever a parameter changes, whether
    from this controller, the front panel, another remote controller, or a
    preset/snapshot recall.

    For MRX7-D, integrators define a Remote Control Setup List in MTX-MRX
    Editor that maps each panel control (fader, mute, selector) to an Index
    number. This driver exposes 32 Index slots; bind your panel UI to the
    set_index / inc_index / query_index commands.

    For MTX3 / MTX5-D / XMV / EXi8 / EXo8, addresses look like
    MTX:mem_512/60000/0/0/0/0/0 - the seven-element path identifies a
    specific channel/parameter. Look up addresses in section 7 ("Parameter
    List") of the RCP specification, then use set_param / get_param /
    inc_param.
  setup: >
    1. Connect the Yamaha device(s) to the network via Ethernet.
    2. In MTX-MRX Editor (or ProVisionaire Edit for newer firmware), open
       System -> Remote Control. Enable network remote control on TCP port
       49280. The device supports up to 8 simultaneous remote sessions.
    3. (MRX7-D only) In MTX-MRX Editor, open Remote Control Setup List.
       Assign each panel control to an Index number 1..32. Synchronize the
       project to the device.
    4. Enter the device's IP address in the OpenAVC device config. Leave
       port at 49280.
    5. Optional: set keepalive_timeout > 0 to enable RCP keepalive for
       unreliable networks. The device will drop the connection if no
       command arrives within the configured timeout.

discovery:
  active_probes:
    - yamaha_rcp
  oui_prefixes:
    - "00:a0:de"   # Yamaha Corporation

default_config:
  host: ""
  port: 49280
  poll_interval: 30
  inter_command_delay: 0.05
  keepalive_timeout: 0

config_schema:
  host:
    type: string
    required: true
    label: IP Address
  port:
    type: integer
    default: 49280
    label: TCP Port
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 30
    min: 0
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
    description: "Refresh backstop. Set to 0 to rely entirely on push notifications."
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.05
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
  keepalive_timeout:
    type: integer
    default: 0
    min: 0
    label: Keepalive Timeout (ms)
    description: "0 disables. Recommended only for unreliable networks. The device will drop the connection if no command arrives within this window."

state_variables:
  device_runmode:
    type: string
    label: Run Mode
  device_error:
    type: string
    label: Last Alert
  device_fs:
    type: string
    label: Sample Rate
  device_lockstatus:
    type: string
    label: Word Clock Status
  protocol_version:
